        <div id="ac-wattage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. AC Output Limits: Why 100W, 300W and 600W Are Different Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every battery with an outlet has an inverter inside, a small circuit that turns the battery's low-voltage DC into 120-volt AC. The inverter's continuous rating is the single most important number on the box, because it sets the ceiling on what you can plug in. Exceed it and the unit shuts off its outlet to protect itself. Capacity tells you how long something runs; inverter wattage tells you whether it runs at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Tiers of Output</h3>

          <p>
            The products on the market fall into three rough tiers. True power banks with an outlet, like the mophie powerstation pro AC, top out around 60 to 100W. That covers a laptop brick, a camera charger or a lamp, and nothing much bigger. The second tier is the 300W class, which includes the EcoFlow River 3, the Jackery Explorer 240 v2 and the Anker SOLIX C300. Those handle a CPAP, a small TV, a router and modem, a fan and most laptop chargers, often several at once. The third tier is the 600W class, like the BLUETTI EB3A and EcoFlow River 3 Plus, which adds blenders, a mini fridge or a power tool charger.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">AC Output by Tier</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">60-100W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">True power banks with an outlet</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">300W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Small LiFePO4 stations, 245-288Wh</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">600W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Larger compact stations, 268-286Wh</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1,500W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">What a hair dryer or space heater needs</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surge Ratings</h3>

          <p>
            Surge ratings matter for anything with a motor or compressor. A mini fridge that runs at 80W may draw 400 to 600W for a fraction of a second when its compressor starts. A 300W unit with a 600W surge rating may catch it, or may not, depending on the fridge. A 100W power bank never will. If a device has a motor, look at the surge figure first and plan around it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Boost Modes Explained</h3>

          <p>
            The modes that boost output, EcoFlow's X-Boost and BLUETTI's Power Lifting, deserve a plain explanation. They let the inverter run a resistive heating device rated above its limit by lowering the voltage. A 1,000W kettle on X-Boost might draw 600W and take nearly twice as long to boil. That is useful in a pinch for a kettle or a hot plate. It does nothing for motors, compressors or electronics, and some devices simply refuse to work at reduced voltage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy the Tier That Covers Your Load</h3>

          <p>
            The practical rule is to add up the watts of everything you will run at once, add 20 percent headroom, and buy the tier that covers that number. For most people asking for a power bank with an outlet, the answer is the 300W class. If your list includes a CPAP with a heated humidifier, a mini fridge or a blender, step up to 600W. If your list is just a laptop and a phone, you may not need an outlet at all, which we cover below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pure-sine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Pure Sine Wave and Why It Matters
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pure vs Modified Sine Wave</h3>

          <p>
            Grid power arrives as a smooth, rounded wave. Cheap inverters approximate that wave with stepped blocks, which is called modified sine wave. Pure sine wave inverters reproduce the smooth curve closely. Every power station on this list, the River 3, River 3 Plus, Jackery 240 v2, Anker C300 and BLUETTI EB3A, is rated as pure sine wave, and that is one of the main reasons to buy a known brand rather than a no-name pack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Devices Care</h3>

          <p>
            Most laptop bricks and phone chargers tolerate modified sine wave because they convert AC back to DC immediately. The trouble starts with devices that use the AC wave directly. CPAP machines, some medical equipment, variable-speed fans, audio gear, battery chargers for power tools, and anything with a small motor can run hot, buzz, behave erratically or refuse to start. Some CPAP makers specifically recommend pure sine wave for battery operation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Modified Sine Wave Hides</h3>

          <p>
            The cheap AC power banks that flood online marketplaces are where modified sine wave still hides. A listing may say "AC outlet" or "110V" and never mention the waveform, which is usually a tell. Those packs also tend to quote large mAh figures that do not translate into much usable AC power, and their 100W or 150W claims may be peak rather than continuous. If a listing does not state pure sine wave clearly, assume it is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Pure Sine Wave Does and Does Not Fix</h3>

          <p>
            Pure sine wave does not make a battery safe for every load. It still has to stay under its wattage limit, and it still runs out of energy. But it removes the most common reason people report that a device "won't work on the battery" or that a fan sounds wrong. For anyone buying a battery for a CPAP, we treat pure sine wave as a requirement, not a feature.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more point: the waveform only matters when you use the outlet. Power drawn through USB-C or a 12V car socket never passes through the inverter, so it is unaffected. That is one reason we keep recommending USB-C first for any device that accepts it.
          </p>
        </div>

