          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heating Devices: The Bottom Row</h3>

          <p>
            The bottom row is where most disappointment comes from. Anything that makes heat with electricity, including coffee makers, toasters, hair dryers, curling irons, electric blankets on high and space heaters, draws far more than any battery on this list can sustain. Even when a boost mode lets one run at reduced power, a 1,500W heater would empty a 288Wh battery in about ten minutes. Our guide to <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-space-heater/">running a space heater on a power station</a> walks through that math in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fridges and CPAP Machines</h3>

          <p>
            Fridges sit in a gray zone. A small, efficient fridge may run on a 600W station, and a 300W unit may handle a very efficient dorm fridge, but the compressor startup surge is the hurdle and the runtime is short. A full-size kitchen fridge needs a much larger battery, which is covered in our guide to <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">whether a power station can run a refrigerator</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            CPAP machines are the most common serious reason people buy one of these. A CPAP without its heated humidifier often runs at 30 to 60W, which any 300W station handles, and many CPAPs accept a DC cable that skips the inverter altogether. The humidifier can double or triple consumption. Our <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/">CPAP runtime guide</a> has nightly figures by battery size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch the Display When in Doubt</h3>

          <p>
            When in doubt, plug the device in and watch the battery's display. Every station on this list shows live output in watts, and a few minutes of watching tells you more than any spec sheet. If the number is close to the limit, the battery will be working hard and will drain quickly.
          </p>
