        <div id="runtime-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Runtime Math That Actually Works
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity, Load and Losses</h3>

          <p>
            Runtime is capacity divided by load, with a deduction for losses. The inverter itself costs energy, usually 10 to 15 percent, and the battery management system holds back a little reserve. A reasonable planning figure is that you get about 85 percent of a LiFePO4 station's rated watt-hours out of its AC outlet. For a lithium-ion power bank like the mophie, plan on closer to 75 to 80 percent through the outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Math on a 288Wh Station</h3>

          <p>
            Here is how that works in practice. A 288Wh Anker C300 delivers about 245Wh through its outlet. A 60W laptop brick charging a laptop runs about four hours, which is usually two or three full laptop charges because a laptop does not draw full power the whole time. A 20W router and modem combination runs about 12 hours. A CPAP without humidifier drawing 30 to 45W runs about five and a half to eight hours, which is roughly one night, so check your own machine's draw before you count on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Math on a True Power Bank</h3>

          <p>
            The same math is unkind to the true power banks. The mophie holds just under 100Wh. Through its outlet, expect roughly 75Wh, which is a single full charge of many 14-inch laptops, or about three to four hours of a small router. That is plenty for a flight or a coffee-shop afternoon. It is not much for a two-day outage, which is why our <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">guide to portable chargers for power outages</a> leans toward larger batteries for emergency use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Idle Draw and a Fuller Worksheet</h3>

          <p>
            Idle draw is the hidden cost. An inverter switched on with nothing plugged in still uses a few watts, and over a day that can waste a meaningful slice of a 250Wh battery. Turn the AC output off when you are not using it, and use USB-C or the 12V socket for small loads overnight. Most of these units have an auto-off setting for the inverter; enable it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a fuller worksheet, including how to total several devices across a day, our guide to <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">what size power station you need</a> walks through the same method with larger batteries. The short version is that if your daily total is under 200Wh, anything on this list works. If it is 300 to 800Wh, you need the expandable River 3 Plus or a bigger station.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bank-vs-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Power Bank or Small Power Station?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Line Has Blurred</h3>

          <p>
            The line between a power bank with an outlet and a small power station has blurred almost completely. A decade ago, products like the original Omnicharge and RAVPower AC banks filled this niche with lithium-ion packs under 100Wh. Most of those are gone. What replaced them are 245Wh to 288Wh LiFePO4 stations that weigh 7 to 10 lb and cost little more than the old banks did, while offering three times the energy and a much stronger inverter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight and Travel Decide It</h3>

          <p>
            So the real question is weight and travel. If you need to fly with it, or carry it in a laptop bag all day, a true power bank under 100Wh is the only option, and the mophie powerstation pro AC is one of the few left from a major brand. If it lives in a car, a closet, a tent or next to a bed, a small station is better in every way that matters: more energy, a stronger pure sine inverter, LiFePO4 longevity and better cost per watt-hour.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule of Thumb</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Under 100Wh if it flies. 250Wh or more if it doesn't.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A small LiFePO4 station gives you roughly three times the energy of an AC power bank for a similar price and about four times the weight. Unless the airline limit or a laptop bag rules it out, the station wins.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Chemistry</h3>

          <p>
            Chemistry is the other big difference. Most power banks use lithium-ion cells rated for a few hundred to around a thousand cycles. Every station on this list uses LiFePO4, rated for thousands of cycles and more tolerant of long storage. If the battery will sit in a closet waiting for storms, that tolerance matters more than the cycle count. Our explainer on <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion power stations</a> covers the tradeoffs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When You Have Outgrown the Category</h3>

          <p>
            Once your needs go beyond about 300Wh a day, you have left this category entirely. A fridge, a CPAP with humidifier for several nights or a sump pump all point toward a 1,000Wh class unit, and our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power station guide</a> is the place to start. Buying a small station and expecting it to do that job is the most common regret we see from owners.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="usb-c-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Use USB-C Before the Outlet
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Outlet Wastes Energy</h3>

          <p>
            The most effective way to get more runtime out of any battery with an outlet is to use the outlet less. When you plug a laptop brick into the AC outlet, the battery converts DC to AC in its inverter, then the brick converts AC back to DC for the laptop. Each conversion wastes energy, and together they can throw away 15 to 25 percent of what the battery holds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Charge Over USB-C Instead</h3>

          <p>
            Plug the same laptop into a USB-C port instead and that double conversion disappears. The Anker C300's 140W USB-C ports and the Jackery 240 v2's 100W USB-C port can charge most current laptops at full speed. On a 288Wh battery, switching a laptop from the outlet to USB-C can easily add the equivalent of another full laptop charge over a weekend.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DC Cords for CPAPs and Other Devices</h3>

          <p>
            The same logic applies to other devices. Many CPAP makers sell a DC power cord that runs from a 12V socket, which avoids the inverter and often lets you skip the heated humidifier on battery nights. Phones and tablets should always use USB. Many routers run on 12V DC internally, and a 12V cable or a USB-C to DC adapter can run them more efficiently than their wall brick does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip the Outlet, and Switch It Off</h3>

          <p>
            This is also why some buyers do not need an outlet at all. If every device you own charges over USB-C, a high-wattage USB-C power bank or a DC-only station does the same job lighter and cheaper. Keep the outlet for the things that genuinely need it: older laptops with barrel plugs, CPAP humidifiers, camera battery chargers, a small TV, a lamp or a fan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn the inverter off when you switch a device to USB-C. Leaving the AC output on with nothing plugged in still costs a few watts every hour, which defeats part of the purpose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="travel-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Flying, Storage and Safety
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flying: The 100Wh Line</h3>

          <p>
            Airlines in the US follow FAA guidance that lithium batteries up to 100Wh can go in carry-on bags without approval, batteries from 100 to 160Wh need airline approval, and anything larger is generally not allowed in the cabin or checked luggage. That puts every power station on this list, from 245Wh up, off limits for flying. The mophie, at just under 100Wh, is the only pick here that travels by air. Power banks of any size are never allowed in checked bags.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storage</h3>

          <p>
            For storage, LiFePO4 stations are forgiving. A good routine is to store them at 50 to 80 percent charge in a cool, dry room, check them every three months and top them up before storm season. Lithium-ion power banks lose charge faster and age faster at high temperatures, so do not leave one in a hot car through the summer and expect it to be healthy in the fall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Indoor Use</h3>

          <p>
            Battery stations produce no exhaust and are safe to use indoors, which is the main reason to buy one over a small gas generator for apartment or bedroom use. Keep the vents clear, do not cover the unit with blankets, and keep it off carpet while it is charging at high speed. Our guide to <a href="/are-portable-power-stations-safe-indoors/">whether power stations are safe indoors</a> covers ventilation and placement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cheap Packs and Sustained High Loads</h3>

          <p>
            The real safety risk in this category is the cheap no-name AC power bank. Packs with inflated capacity claims, missing safety certifications and unnamed cells are the ones most likely to swell, overheat or fail under load. If you would trust a device to run a CPAP next to your bed, buy it from a brand with a support line and a warranty. Look for UL or equivalent safety listings on the product page.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never plug a power strip with a space heater or a hair dryer into a battery and walk away. Even with overload protection, sustained high loads heat the inverter and the battery, and they drain small units in minutes. Use the outlet for small electronics and lighting, and let the bigger loads wait for a bigger power source.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Choose in Five Questions
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will You Fly With It or Carry It All Day?</h3>

          <p>
            First, do you need to fly with it or carry it all day? If yes, you need a true power bank under 100Wh, and the mophie powerstation pro AC is the pick. Accept the 100W ceiling and use USB-C wherever you can. If no, move to a small station, because it gives you far more for the money.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Is the Biggest Thing You Will Plug In?</h3>

          <p>
            Second, what is the biggest single thing you will plug in? If it is a laptop, a router, a CPAP without humidifier or a lamp, a 300W unit like the Anker SOLIX C300, EcoFlow River 3 or Jackery Explorer 240 v2 is enough. If it is a CPAP with humidifier plus a laptop, a mini fridge, a blender or a tool charger, buy a 600W unit like the BLUETTI EB3A or EcoFlow River 3 Plus.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Many Hours Do You Need?</h3>

          <p>
            Third, how many hours do you need? Add up your daily watt-hours and divide by 0.85. If the result is under about 240Wh, any station here will do. If it is closer to 400 to 800Wh, buy the River 3 Plus and its add-on battery, or step up to a 1,000Wh class unit from our <a href="/best-ecoflow-power-station/">EcoFlow</a> or <a href="/best-jackery-power-station/">Jackery</a> roundups.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">UPS Function and Solar Charging</h3>

          <p>
            Fourth, will it run while plugged into the wall? If you want it to protect a router or desktop through brief outages, buy one with a rated UPS function. The EcoFlow River 3 and River 3 Plus are the clearest choices, with switchover rated under 20ms and under 10ms respectively.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fifth, will you charge it with solar? If so, the BLUETTI EB3A's 200W solar input is the strongest here. Pair any of these with a folding panel from our <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">portable solar panel guide</a>, and match the panel's wattage to the station's solar input limit rather than buying the biggest panel you can find.
          </p>
        </div>

