        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best power bank with an AC outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Anker SOLIX C300 is the best pick: 288Wh of LiFePO4, a 300W pure sine outlet and two 140W USB-C ports in a carry-friendly size. If you need to fly with it, the mophie powerstation pro AC is one of the few true power banks with an outlet under the 100Wh carry-on limit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power bank with an outlet run a CPAP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it has a pure sine inverter and enough capacity. A CPAP without humidifier usually draws 30 to 60W, so a 245 to 288Wh station covers roughly one night, longer on DC power. With a heated humidifier, consumption can double or triple, so consider a 600W expandable unit or a DC cord that skips the inverter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts do I need in a battery pack with an outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add the watts of everything you will run at the same time and add about 20 percent headroom. Laptops, routers, lamps and CPAPs without humidifiers fit a 300W unit. A mini fridge, blender or CPAP plus laptop together points to 600W. Anything that heats with electricity needs far more than this category offers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power bank with an AC outlet allowed on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is 100Wh or less, which allows it in carry-on bags without airline approval under FAA guidance. Batteries between 100 and 160Wh need airline approval, and larger units are not allowed. Power banks are never permitted in checked luggage. Every 245Wh-plus power station on this list stays home.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power outlet run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not for long. A small efficient fridge may start and run on a 600W station, but the compressor startup surge can exceed 400W and a 288Wh battery would only last several hours. A full-size kitchen fridge needs a 1,000Wh class power station or larger, ideally with solar or wall recharging between runs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a power bank with an outlet and a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly size and chemistry. AC power banks are usually under 100Wh with lithium-ion cells and 60 to 100W outlets. Small power stations start around 245Wh, use longer-lived LiFePO4 cells and offer 300 to 600W pure sine output. Stations cost more per unit but much less per watt-hour, and they do not fly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is pure sine wave necessary for a plug in battery pack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For laptops and phone chargers, not strictly, since they convert power to DC immediately. For CPAPs, fans, tool chargers, audio gear and anything with a motor, yes. Modified sine wave can cause heat, buzzing or failure to start. Every station on our list is rated pure sine wave, which is one reason to avoid no-name packs.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by mAh instead of watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A listing that shouts 60,000mAh says nothing about how much the outlet can deliver. Check two numbers: watt-hours for capacity and continuous AC watts for the outlet limit. A 100W outlet will not run a 150W device for any length of time, no matter how many milliamp-hours the box claims.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting it to run heating appliances</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coffee makers, hair dryers, toasters and space heaters draw 800 to 1,500W. No power bank and no station on this list can sustain that. Boost modes run some at reduced power, but a small battery drains in minutes. Plan heat and cooking around gas, propane outdoors or a much larger battery.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the waveform</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cheap AC packs often use modified sine wave and do not say so. CPAP machines, fans, tool chargers and audio gear can run hot, buzz or refuse to start on it. If a listing does not clearly state pure sine wave, assume it is not, and do not trust it with medical equipment.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running laptops through the outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plugging a USB-C laptop into its wall brick and the brick into the battery wastes 15 to 25 percent of the energy in two conversions. Use the battery's USB-C port directly whenever your laptop supports it. You get more runtime, less heat and less fan noise from the station.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the inverter on all night</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An inverter with nothing plugged in still draws a few watts. Over a day or two, that quietly eats a real share of a 250Wh battery. Switch off the AC output when you are done, enable auto-off if the unit has it, and use USB or 12V for small overnight loads.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

